 "whatWasRecoverable": "everything that could be seated was: 188 landed and verified byte-identical, plus the nine bound to the projection. These are the residue.",
 "andWHYNOTREDERIVABLE": "an execution is authored prose about one specific option (R922), not a computation. This store cannot write them and there is no longer a store that can.",
 "theRetentionChoice": "git-tracked at content/standing-losses/ rather than only staged. A loss recorded in one delivered file is itself single-holder, which is the fault that produced it (R951).",
 "THE_CAUSES_WERE_RESTATED_AFTER_INVESTIGATION": {
  "why": "the first draft of this record named causes that were true of the LOOKUP and false of the corpus. Each was investigated before being written down as permanent, because a standing loss is a claim that something is gone forever and a wrong reason on it is worse than no record.",
  "firstFraming": "\"no option on this row resolves to that identity\" — true of the id, and 21 of those questions were present under other ids.",
  "secondFraming": "\"the row under this id is a different question here\" — true, and still not the cause: matched on pairA and stemWord, the questions WERE found.",
  "THE_ACTUAL_CAUSE": "the OPTION the execution was written about no longer exists. gen-vr-03-related-words: the artefact's execution for kitten/cat -> puppy names option \"bone\"; this store's row for that exact question carries \"paw\" and \"whiskers\". **The distractors were regenerated after the executions were authored.** The question survives, the seat does not, and an execution is prose about a seat.",
  "andWHYTHATMATTERSFORTHERECORD": "a loss recorded as \"id mismatch\" invites someone to fix the ids and expect recovery. A loss recorded as \"the seat was regenerated away\" tells them the truth: there is nothing to point at."
 }
